Huh. I don't see "loqban" anywhere on lojban.org. Is this writing system=20
your invention, Tobias?

I understand that Lojban's Latin-based orthography seems a little weird at=
=20
first. But to me, using *q* for the IPA /=CA=92/ seems stranger than things=
 like=20
using the apostrophe for <h>. The apostrophe was an odd choice, but the=20
light-weight character does fit use of {y'y} as a "semiconsonant" serving=
=20
only as a vowel separator. Also, people who don't like Lojban's usage of=20
{.} and {'} can already just omit the dot and replace the apostrophe with=
=20
an *h* without causing any confusion. And I'm not sure spelling "Johan" as=
=20
{.johan} instead of {.io'an.} or {iohan} is a significant gain. Rather than=
=20
trying to "fix" Lojban's current official usage of the Latin alphabet, I'd=
=20
be more interested in officially adopting either a new script or one of the=
 original=20
scripts listed on the Alternate Orthographies page=20
<https://mw.lojban.org/papri/Alternate_orthographies#Original_Scripts>...=
=20
With the currently used scripts from natural languages like Latin and=20
Cyrillic remaining acceptable transliterations.

~Andrew / cemjig


On Friday, September 16, 2016 at 1:10:35 PM UTC-7, Tobias Conradi wrote:
>
> The loqban writing system for Lojban has been published.=20
>
> It uses 26 characters of ISO 10646 Basic Latin.=20
>
> Diphthongs use <w> and <j> (aj, aw, ej, oj, ja, je, ji, jo, ju, wa, we,=
=20
> wi, wo, wu, jy, wy).=20
>
> <h> is used in place of <'> for IPA /h/.=20
> <q> is used for IPA /=CA=92/, <c> for IPA /=CA=83/ and <y> for IPA /=C9=
=99/.=20
>
> This writing system increases the agreement with IPA. It allows for a=20
> Lojban pronunciation of 1 000 000+ taxon names used in scientific naming =
of=20
> biota, without changing their spelling.=20
>
> It can also be used to spell names like .johan zebastjan bax., .pjotr=20
> iljitc tcajkovski. and .dqenghis xan.
>

--=20
